# Dashboard composition and editing

Make it obvious whether someone is editing the dashboard or using it. Editing changes the interface configuration. Using a dashboard can operate devices. When the appearance changes, keep the existing cards, entity targets, visibility conditions and unsaved changes exactly as they were.

These examples are simulated with invented data. They do not change a dashboard or control a home. This page covers the editing contract for gap HA-GAP-022. [Dashboard composition and card features](dashboard-composition.html) owns which layout or card to choose.

## Scope and native ownership

Native Sections views arrange cards in a grid and provide editing, placement and visibility. This page sets task and acceptance rules around the native editor. It does not add a civica layout editor, and it does not promise a keyboard alternative that has not been verified. Source: [Sections view](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/sections/), reviewed on 11 November 2026.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| View mode | Household controls and navigation. | Show reorder handles that look like device controls. |
| Edit mode | The dashboard and the object being edited. | Let a move activate the card’s main device action. |
| Draft changed | The edited configuration, kept apart from the saved version. | Label a preview as saved. |
| Validation rejected | The issue beside its card or field, with the supported draft kept. | Discard unrelated card settings. |
| Save result uncertain | The draft kept, and a check of the stored version. | Overwrite a concurrent edit without looking. |

## Integration contract

1. Let the native editor own its configuration and saving. A theme change must not serialise, reorder or rewrite saved dashboard records.
2. A card has a type, targets and layout and visibility settings. When editing a supported subset, keep any settings you do not recognise.
3. Say whether a change affects this view, a shared dashboard or one browser. Dashboard visibility does not replace backend authorisation.
4. Use the native move and resize. Where dragging is the only verified route, record that and name a supported editor alternative if there is one. CSS alone cannot make dragging keyboard accessible.

## Accessible interaction

- Name each control with its object, for example “Edit kitchen light card”. Keep a logical focus order when a card moves or is removed.
- Check that dense placement does not make the visual order contradict the reading and keyboard order.
- At 320px, enlarged text and touch zoom, keep Save, Cancel and validation reachable without covering a live device action.

## Failure and recovery

- If an entity disappears, keep its configured identifier and explain that the target is invalid. Do not replace the card silently.
- If a save is rejected, keep the supported draft edits and the native validation. After an uncertain response, compare with the stored version.
- Warn about the documented consequences of taking over an automatically maintained dashboard. Do not promise a one-click reversal unless one exists.

